[#:seon.task{:name "a-check-step-is-a-task",
             :plan
             "What exists: bin/check's steps run as a script (`seon.check`), beside the reconciler. Correct: each step is a task in resources/seon/tasks/seon.check.edn carrying :seon.task/argv and :needs; the reconciler runs it as a child through one effect owner (`seon.fx`, an exclusive step running alone); its outcome is the run's end facts (exit, signal, output path); the run ends on the quiet fact and names the red step with what it blocks. One machinery for agents and checks; replaces the check script's own ordering. Shown in a prototype (e7_one_system): 9 steps in 1.46 s against 1.9 s serial, exclusive steps never overlapped. A first implementation on an older base is jolt/runner b4ea59d (reference only: :check tasks, an :fx/proc child owner, deftests a-red-check-step-blocks-only-its-dependents, a-check-step-passes-by-its-own-expected-exit, an-exclusive-child-runs-alone).",
             :kind :design,
             :needs
             #{"clojure.core.async.flow/a-graph-counts-its-messages-and-says-when-it-is-quiet"},
             :proofs
             #{seon.check-test/check-steps-run-by-needs-and-end-on-quiet}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-refused-flight-write-is-not-ignored"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: every flight's write (:spawned, :started, :ran, :octopus-merged, then :done) goes through the writer, and the :done step of `seon.reconcile/advance` is `(done st)`: it drops the answer. Reproduction (jolt/runner e0efb69): the store hit MDB_MAP_FULL, the writer refused a run's end (`seon.writer/writer-one` answers :seon.writer/refused), the run stayed :running, the reconciler never reached quiet and nothing said why. Correct: an answer that is an error map is read: the flight records it where it can (the task's :seon.task/error by a smaller write, or the reconciler's state and ping-map as :refused with the run and message when main refuses every write), and quiescence counts the flight as ended with that error, so the run ends and names the error. The refused spawn (above) covers spawns only. A first fix on :check tasks is jolt/runner b880791 (WIP); its proof is re-targeted to an agent's run."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.reconcile-test/a-refused-end-write-is-reported-at-quiet}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-octopus-merge-is-proven-by-one-combined-run"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: octopus merges (the reconciler's :octopus-merge op) and reach selection (`seon.agent/run-tests-forked`), kept off (:seon.config.merge/max-running is 1 in default.edn) until this exists. Correct: an octopus merge's members are proven together by one combined run of the tests their changes reach; on red the members are bisected (halves re-run) to name the one that broke it, and the green members merge. Replaces the off switch. Reference only: jolt/r5-port 302ce17 (parked, on an old base; the archived paper option3-R5-octopus (git history) merges.md); re-derive on main's reconciler."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.reconcile-test/a-red-octopus-merge-is-bisected-to-its-member}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-design-is-done-when-its-proofs-are-designed-red"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `seon.reconcile/done-explain` judges a :design run only by its plan changing (:no-design). Reproduction (agent-work audit): 4 of 4 designs wrote full bodies and no cost proof, and two passed as done while registering a 3-value status enum over 10 stored values. Correct (DESIGN-tasks §7, prototyped): design is done when a task needing it holds at least one proof and each such proof is designed red: every report :pass or :fail, or an :error whose ex-data carries :seon.error/type (a stub's :seon.error/unimplemented), with at least one not a pass. An untyped error (No such var) or an all-green proof is not designed red: error :not-designed-red naming the proof. Deletes: nothing; :no-design stays."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.task/design-writes-the-implement-steps-proofs"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.reconcile-test/a-design-is-refused-until-its-proofs-are-designed-red}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-unreached-change-refuses-the-last-task"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `seon.reconcile/done-explain` refuses red, unknown and stray tests, regressions, contract gaps and unmerged entries, but not a change no test reaches. v1's done rule refused 'functions no green test covers' at the last task of a chain, and its ``my.test/check`` answered :seon.test/unreached. Correct: at a task that carries :seon.task/target (the merge), every var in `seon.merge/divergence` against the branch's base that no green test's reach covers (the reach `seon.proofs/plan` selects by, over `seon.program/graph-index`) refuses {:error :uncovered :symbol s}; the context names each with the form that lists what reaches it. Deletes nothing."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.reconcile-test/an-unreached-change-refuses-at-the-merge}}
 {:seon.task/name "root-work-is-tasks"
  :seon.task/plan "PLANNED, NOT BUILT: waits for root's go. The agent loop's park and send parts are built (`docs/turn.md`, `seon.turn/work!`, `seon.turn/hand-back!`). Owner, 2026-10-25 (docs/decisions.md: root is main; root's special cases go): root's own work is tasks run by agents on main's current commit, never a human-only sink.

What exists. A failure is `seon.reconcile/hold-tx`: the task `:held` with `:seon.task/failure {:reason :run}` and `:seon.task/evidence` (`seon.reconcile/evidence`: conflicts, unmerged, reds, problem), the run `:closed`. Its causes are `seon.reconcile/hold-tx`'s callers: `seon.reconcile/actions`' `failed` (the done rule refused, or the agent died) and the `:octopus-merged` step (a merge refused other than `:behind`). Nobody owns a held task: `seon.reconcile/root-causes` lists them, and `seon.reconcile/quiet` writes that list into ONE fact, `:seon.root/quiescent` on the root entity, which every later quiet spell overwrites, so a cause nobody read before the next spell is gone. Two more root failures are not even holds: a health problem a merge caused on main (`seon.problems/caused`, judged per branch before its merge, never on the octopus's combined result), and a failed build (`:seon.root/generation` `{:error}`, again one overwritten fact). Templates exist only for work kinds (`seon.template/instructions`: merge, design, implement, test).

Which failures become tasks, given the agent loop. Under the loop design a `:waiting`, `:cap`, `:no-progress` or provider-`:error` end parks with no hold, and a refused done-rule end holds and parks with its reason in the user's timeline (the loop's owner question 2): both stay the session's own, not root's. Root's are the five the owner named: a refused merge, a dead agent, health a merge caused, red proofs on main, a failed update.

The change (one mechanism, `seon.template`'s request path reused).
1. Five templates in `seon.template/instructions`, kinds `:root-merge :agent-died :health :red-proofs :update`, task namespace `root`, model and budgets from config like any task (`seon.reconcile/desired-env`): no kind switch in the reconciler; the agent's instructions say what to read and what ends it.
2. One new function in seon.template, root-task, from a failure (kind, the evidence map, the failed task and run, or main's t for health/red/update) to one task declaration. Its id is the cause's own: `root/merge-<run>`, `root/died-<run>`, `root/health-<main t>`, `root/red-<test>@<digest>`, `root/update-<commit>`. So the same failure seen twice is the same task (an upsert of identical facts) and two failures are two tasks: nothing overwrites.
3. Each producer writes the task in the SAME transaction as the fact of the failure: `seon.reconcile/hold-tx` (merge refused, agent died) gains the task's datoms; `seon.prove`'s result write for main gains a `:red-proofs` task when a test turned red at a digest that was green; the octopus merge's `:committed` write gains a `:health` task when `seon.problems/caused` of main after vs before is non-empty (cost to measure first: the per-branch `:caused` already runs in each judge); `my.root/build!`'s generation write gains an `:update` task on `{:error}`. One write per failure, as `request-tx` writes a linked set.
4. The task runs like any task: the reconciler spawns its agent forked from main's committed head (main's current program: the fork server holds it, `program/load-changes!`), on its own branch `root/<id>` (its writes, if any, merge by its own merge task, `with-merge`, through the one merge path). A root task's agent never holds another session's branch.
5. How each relates to the failed task (the parked agent of the loop design is never killed by it):
   - `:root-merge`: the failed merge task is held and its agent parked on its branch. The root task's agent reads the evidence and the branch (read-only: `my.branch` reads of another branch), then acts through the one send: a message to that session (wakes it, with the conflicts and what to do) or `my.task/fail!` of the original with a reason. Messaging a held task from root releases it in the same transaction (loop design section 3). The root task ends done when it has sent its decision; the original's own next run is judged as any run.
   - `:agent-died`: the run is `:dead` and the session has no process (loop design: the next message resumes it at its head). The root task reads exit status, stderr tail and `last-op`; it resumes the session by a message, or files a platform task (a defect in what died), or fails the original.
   - `:health`, `:red-proofs`: work tasks on main's current commit: make the problem entries or the reds go away, proved by `seon.problems/caused` empty and the reds green; their fix merges by the merge task like any work.
   - How a `:health` task closes when its cause is gone: no new rule; its proof is the problem entry it names reading `:open? false` or being absent (`seon.problems/closed?`: a fact written on the error's subject after its last occurrence). So an entry a live proc makes untrue closes the task without an agent's fix: the reconciler's observe sees the proof true and the task done, and the spawn it would have made is not made. That needs every recorded error to name a subject whose later write means its cause is gone. A refused build names one (`my.root/build!`: :seon.root/generation). A health task for an entry with no such subject is the agent's to judge: it reads the cause, and ends the task either with a fix or with `my.task/fail!` and its reason.
   - `:update`: the build's error is its evidence; fix on main as work, or the build input that failed.
6. No recursion: a root task's own failure (its merge refused, its agent dead) is held and parked for the user, with no root task of a root task (the template reads the failed task's ns: `root` gets none). That is the one human sink left, at the top.
7. Deleted: the `:root-causes` member of `:seon.root/quiescent` and its write in `seon.reconcile/quiet` (the quiet spell keeps its `{:t}` fact: the signal tests and `seon.await` wait on, an event, not a store of causes); `seon.reconcile/root-causes` stays only as the render of root's page (the open `root/*` tasks and the held tasks that block released work), reading tasks, storing nothing; the `{:error}` overwrite of `:seon.root/generation` stays as the build's answer, the task is its record.

Correctness: every failure is a durable task with an owner, a run, proofs and an end; two failures keep two causes; nothing a later spell writes erases one. Perf: one write per failure (the task's datoms ride the failure's own transaction); the health check of a merge is its one added cost, to measure (estimate: one `seon.problems/caused` over main before and after the octopus, which the judge already does per branch). Lines: about +120 (templates, root-task, producers) and -40 (root-causes storage, quiescent's causes): a capability, net up, complexity down.

Proofs (to write red first): two failures one after the other (a refused merge, then a dead agent, with a quiet spell between and after) keep both causes, each as a `root/*` task, after every later quiet spell; the same failure observed twice is one task; a red proof on main makes one `:red-proofs` task naming the test and digest; a root task's own failure makes no root task; a root task's agent forks from main's head at its spawn (its program digest equals main's), on its own branch.

Questions for root: (a) admission: do root tasks take places under `:seon.config.agent/max-running` like any task, or go first (a config key, the owner's gate)? Recommended: like any task, ordered first in `seon.reconcile/actions`' starts. (b) does a `:root-merge` message the parked session (recommended: the session has the context) or work on its branch itself (needs the branch free)? (c) health on main per merge (cost measured first) or only when bin/prove's health step reds on main (cheaper, later)?"
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.reconcile-test/two-failures-in-a-row-keep-both-causes-as-tasks
                      seon.reconcile-test/a-failure-seen-twice-is-one-task
                      seon.prove-test/a-red-proof-on-main-is-a-task
                      seon.reconcile-test/a-root-tasks-failure-makes-no-root-task
                      seon.agent-test/a-root-task-forks-from-mains-head
                      seon.reconcile-test/a-health-task-closes-when-its-row-closes}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-gate-that-cannot-run-its-tests-tells-the-agent"
  :seon.task/plan "Seen 2026-11-06 in the kept arm store claude_scratch/context-lab/silence-check/stores/runs-arm-tens: the retraction task (merged since), 7 calls, its done rule satisfied, went to :gate and came back :closed and held, every deferred test in its reach reading `error` (the seon.agent-test list runs to about 4,000 tokens). The agent was never told and 33 turns went unused: the :gate to :closed move in seon.reconcile holds the task for root, where a verdict at the judge wakes the session. Why the tests errored is not established. A gate log of the same arm (merge-tests/705c9b50e6*.tests.log) shows a checkout's build failing with `Could not locate seon/reconcile_test`, but that commit is named for another task, so the link is unproved; read the held reason's tail first, it says why the gate could not run them when the gate itself failed. Correct: a failure the agent can fix reaches it as data (an error is data: docs/errors.md#errors), and one it cannot says so and whose it is, in one line rather than one entry per test. Proof to write: a gate whose tests all read error tells the agent's session once, with the cause. Bears on the context work: this was first misread as a reply with no calls stopping an agent mid-work."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.reconcile-test/gate-tests-all-read-error-tells-agent-s}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-store-made-tasks-merge-reaches-mains-files"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists (2026-11-10, Optimization): a merged task's work reaches main's files by a reviewed sync (docs/source.md#a-merged-task-reaches-mains-files): seon.problems/unsynced? holds a merged task an :unsynced problem while main's files still declare it (:seon.task/declared), my.root/sync! commits its files and its entry's removal onto jolt/sync-<name> from git's main, and the landing's seon.task/load-tx retracts the declaration, closing the row. The gap: a task made in the store with no entry in a file (seon_create, a user's request, a task an agent files) has no :seon.task/declared, so a merge of it that changed Seon's files is never a row, and its work stays on the merges branch only. The cause: the row's closing fact is the entry's removal, which such a task cannot have. The fix to design: a closing fact every sync can make, for instance the landing bridge (bin/land, seon.root/landed-tx) reading the landed message's Completes: lines and writing on each named task that its sync landed, so unsynced? becomes merged and not landed, the entry's retraction one case of it; check first how many merged store-made tasks touched files on the standing store (none would make it no work yet). Cost: one datom a landed task."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.problems-test/a-store-made-merged-task-is-unsynced-until-its-sync-lands}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-refined-plan-is-tried-again-from-main"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner's goal, 2026-11-11: an agent refines its own task after its work, and the refinement is tried from the earlier point, beside the original (docs/tasks.md#a-refine-curates-its-own-task). What exists: ```seon.task/carry-tx``` carries the task content a branch changed to main at its run's end (docs/tasks.md#task-content-reaches-main-at-the-runs-end), so a refined plan needs no new attribute to reach main. Plan: two rules of the one reconciler. First: a work task whose run ended holding it or finishing it starts its refine on the work's own branch (the refine reads the session it curates). The refine cites the work task and needs nothing: a need means finished before I start, and a refine is wanted whatever the outcome (a refine that needed a failed task was never ready). It reads and edits the work task's plan at its cite's path (seon.ctx-test/a-cited-task-is-held-with-what-may-be-edited-there, branch jolt/ctxids). No refine for a parked agent (it goes on), for a refine's own end, or for a task whose plan nobody wrote (a merge task's is the system's), read from who wrote the plan. Second: a task not merged whose plan on main changed since its last run started, by a refine, runs again fresh from main. A refine is known by its cite of the refine section and is judged by the plan it leaves (an outcome of no design when it leaves none). Open, not in this task: a task already merged cannot be run again from where it started, because its start point is not data. Proof, red first: on a planted world a work task ends, its refine runs on its branch and edits the plan, main holds the edited plan at the refine's end, and one fresh run of the work task starts from main with it; a refine that changes nothing starts no run."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.reconcile-test/every-end-gets-one-refine-and-a-refine-gets-none seon.reconcile-test/a-refined-plan-starts-one-fresh-run-from-main}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-merge-step-is-read-from-needs-and-branch"
  :seon.task/plan "A trial (Point's yes, 2026-11-11): there are no kinds (docs/data-modeling.md#a-thing-is-its-attributes), and 20 sites branch on the task's kind. Eleven of them ask one question, is this a merge step, and the data that answers it exists and is read already by the rule that says when a task merges: a task that needs a task on its own branch (the needs and the branch of a task). The sites, the helper's reading, each to be read by the builder before it is changed: src/seon/task.clj 123 (a need left out of the cites a task page shows), 644 (a merge task's plan left out of what later tasks accumulate); src/seon/reconcile.clj 285 (what counts as a merge in flight), 412 (a task behind main is not told to merge when a merge step follows it), 166 with its calls at 433 and 1038 (whether a parked task keeps its process), 478 with its call at 486 (a merge starts when its keys are disjoint from every merge in flight); src/seon/turn.clj 329 with 461 (every turn of a merge step counts toward the merge-turn limit). Plan: one function of a task's needs and branch, the one the merge rule has, read at each site in place of the kind; the same datum says which ends get no refine. Deletes: the eleven kind tests. Wrong if: a site means made by the merge template and not needs a task on its own branch; task.clj 644 is the suspect, since it drops a plan, and may need the plan itself to say what it is. Proof, red first: on a planted world a task that needs a task on its own branch and whose kind says :work is treated at each of these sites as a merge step, and one whose kind says :merge with no such need is not."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.reconcile-test/a-merge-step-is-known-by-its-needs-and-branch}}
 {:seon.task/name "judged-by-the-plan-it-leaves-is-one-datum"
  :seon.task/plan "A trial (Point's yes, 2026-11-11). Eight sites branch on the kind :design, the helper's reading: src/seon/reconcile.clj 65 with its calls at 71 (not done until its plan differs from the plan its branch began with) and 74 (its proofs may be red, never unknown), 370, 371, 406, 877, 878 (the plan before and after handed to the judge); src/seon/ctx/status.clj 262, 277, 278, the agent's own copy of the same rule. The refine has the same rule since tonight: judged by the plan it leaves (docs/tasks.md#a-refine-curates-its-own-task). Two tasks, one rule, so one datum, and the judge and the agent's status read it from one place. Decided (Context, 2026-11-11): the cite. One section of docs/tasks.md states the rule once, and a task is judged so because its plan cites it: a design task's template instructions name it and a refine's declaration names it beside the refine section; one function reads that cite at the eight sites. The other candidate, names no proof of its own and a task on its branch needs it, is wrong: a refine needs nothing and nothing needs it, and it reads behaviour off the shape of a graph that changes when a stage is added. Built as its own tip after the refine chain lands. Deletes: the eight kind tests and the second copy of the rule in the status. Proof, red first: a task with that datum and kind :work is not done until its plan changed and may end with red proofs; one of kind :design without it is judged as any task."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.reconcile-test/a-task-judged-by-its-plan-is-known-by-data}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-refused-start-waits-on-what-frees-it"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: Optimization. Found 2026-11-11 (seon.reconcile/a-refused-start-is-asked-again-when-its-holder-ends fixed one case). A refused start asks again when a commit touches an attribute the reconciler watches (reconcile.clj, the :refused set cleared by any watched commit not only-refused?): a wake-up by a hand-kept list, which misses any end that moves no watched fact. The holder case now waits on the stop's answer; \"too many live agents\" (root's no-room) frees on any agent's end, which moves no watched fact either, so a run at the bound with nothing else committing stays refused. Correct: every refusal carries what frees it and the waiter asks that owner to answer when it is free (root answers a wait on room the way it answers a stop), so the :refused set is cleared by those answers alone and the clear-on-watched-commit rule and only-refused? are deleted. Proof: a start refused for room, with nothing else moving main, starts once an agent ends."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.reconcile-test/a-start-refused-for-room-starts-when-an-agent-ends}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-lost-outside-task-waits-visibly-for-its-agent"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: Optimization. Found 2026-11-11 by Point and Data model on the standing system, read in code and there through seon_eval root: six tasks read :execution :running with :outcome :lost, :seon.task/reason \"root restarted\" (request/add-a-function-demo-math-half-that-halves-a-number-with-a-test-2, tests.inv/agent-checks-13233, -6588, -8207, -9417, tests.inv/agent-path-checks), each one's agent :seon.agent/model \"outside\" (a session through MCP), :dead or :killed. :execution is desired, :outcome observed; the reap (seon.reconcile, :reap) moves :running to :lost at a restart, and the spawn rule leaves lost outside work for an attach on purpose (an outside agent has no turn loop here). Nothing says so: the task reads running and seon_agents lists it as running work. Ruled by Data model, 2026-11-11 (B): :execution keeps its one kind of writer (whoever wants the work: a person, an attach), and the wait is a judgment computed from stored facts, never written: where the summary and the page compute blocked-by and problem, \"waits for an attach: its outside agent was lost at <reason>\", and seon_agents lists it apart from running work. No new attribute. A person who wants it gone writes :held, as for any task. Proof: a root restarted while an outside agent's task runs shows that task waiting for an attach, apart from running work, and an attach then runs it."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.reconcile-test/a-lost-outside-task-waits-visibly-for-its-agent}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-end-whose-proof-is-unknown-is-never-gated"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-12 (loop D2-final, task seon.ctx/stale-section-d0d's fresh rerun, agent dfa947c390088836, loopd2's store): the agent ended its work (a reply with no call) with its own proof never run, was judged :reason \"proof-unknown seon.ctx-test/agent-change-stales-section-reads-status-section-s\", and was still sent to its merge GATE, whose deferred process tests (about 20: seon.agent-test, seon.server-test, seon.repl-test...) ran on a checkout beside a landing's prove, errored on the machine, and closed the task held; the agent spent its last nine turns reading the gate's logs under runs/merge-tests, which it could not act on. A gate judges a branch whose own proofs are green; an end whose proof is unknown is told to run it (a verdict), never gated. Proof: a task ending with its proof unknown gets the proof-unknown verdict and no :gate act."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.reconcile-test/an-end-whose-proof-is-unknown-is-never-gated}}
]
